## Batch Scaling (TASK-003)

UNet and VAE decode measured at batch sizes 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 (100 iterations each,
20 warmup, synthetic random tensors, FP16).

### UNet Batch Scaling

| Batch | Total (ms) | Per-frame (ms) | p99/frame (ms) | Speedup vs batch=1 |
|-------|-----------|----------------|----------------|---------------------|
| 1 | 28.4 | 28.4 | 36.1 | 1.0x |
| 2 | 28.8 | 14.4 | 17.9 | 2.0x |
| 4 | 28.5 | 7.1 | 8.2 | 4.0x |
| 8 | 28.8 | 3.6 | 4.0 | 7.9x |
| 16 | 33.9 | 2.1 | 2.3 | 13.4x |

**UNet scales near-perfectly** up to batch 8 (same total time regardless of batch size,
implying the GPU is severely underutilized at batch=1). Batch 16 shows only 20% overhead
vs batch 1, achieving 13.4x throughput improvement per frame.

### VAE Decode Batch Scaling

| Batch | Total (ms) | Per-frame (ms) | Speedup vs batch=1 |
|-------|-----------|----------------|---------------------|
| 1 | 9.1 | 9.1 | 1.0x |
| 2 | 15.8 | 7.9 | 1.2x |
| 4 | 31.7 | 7.9 | 1.2x |
| 8 | 68.6 | 8.6 | 1.1x |
| 16 | 138.4 | 8.7 | 1.1x |

**VAE decode does NOT benefit from batching.** Cost is nearly linear with batch size
(memory-bandwidth bound). Per-frame cost is flat at ~8-9ms regardless of batch size.

## Decision Gate: Where to Focus Optimization

Based on these measurements, the optimization priority should be:

1. **Blending (49.7ms/frame)** — by far the biggest per-frame cost. Face parsing
   (BiSeNet forward pass) + image compositing. Candidates: torch.compile on BiSeNet,
   pre-compute masks, simplify blending logic, GPU-accelerated compositing.

2. **VAE decode (8.5ms/frame)** — second largest. Does not batch. Candidates:
   torch.compile, TensorRT, reduced precision.

3. **Face detection + landmarks (19.8s one-time)** — large startup cost but amortized
   over session. Candidates: cache coordinates, reduce frame count.

4. **UNet (3.6ms/frame at batch=8)** — already very fast and scales well. Low priority
   for optimization unless targeting sub-2ms latency. Already benefits from SDPA/Flash
   Attention.

